From 10b7c23502e2e69f6a8b6cd50568d9168c4b98bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:23:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan: q6_k mat-vec reads its scales from the block, not
 through LDS - MEASURED NEUTRAL on gfx1103

mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp is the only q*_k mat-vec shader that stages its scales
through shared memory: it loads all 16 int8 scales of the superblock into a
double-buffered LDS tile and pays a barrier() per ROW, inside the row loop.
q4_k and q5_k unpack their scales in registers with no shared memory and no
barrier at all.

The exchange moves no data between threads. The 16 threads of a group all work
on the same block, each writes scales[itid], and each then reads only
scales[s_offset + {0,2,4,6}] of that same block. Reading those four bytes
straight out of the block yields the identical values, in the identical fma
order, off the same 16-byte cache line the staging was already fetching. The
result is bit-identical by construction, and measured so.

Specialization constant 3 selects the path, so ONE binary serves both arms:
default 1 = direct reads, GGML_VK_Q6K_DIRECT_SCALES=0 = upstream's LDS path
byte for byte. The host prints the resolved mode on stderr at pipeline creation
(llama-server's logger drops ggml INFO records, so the receipt has to be on the
same channel the Vulkan perf logger already uses).

MEASURED ON THE SEAT LINE (Radeon 780M / gfx1103, Vulkan, Huihui-Qwen3.8-27B
UD-Q4_K_XL + DFlash2 Q4_K_M drafter at --spec-draft-n-max 2, so verify width 3;
one binary, arms env-gated, 6 position-balanced interleaved launches per phase,
Balanced power plan, 32+16 GB asymmetric RAM):

  op level    q6_k rows median ON/OFF ratio 0.9796, against a CONTROL of 78
              untouched op rows at median 0.9990, p10-p90 [0.952, 1.053].
              No q6_k row separates. NEUTRAL.
  serve level TIED on all six cells - prefill and decode, reported separately,
              at three prompt lengths. Every arm range overlaps.
  identity    byte-identical output: 1 distinct sha256 per prompt across all
              12 arm-launches (3 prompts x 6 launches).
  correctness test-backend-ops -o MUL_MAT 1394/1394, 0 FAIL, both arms.

This does NOT claim a speed win on this box and must not be quoted as one. It
is kept because it is strictly less work for a bit-identical result - one fewer
workgroup barrier per row per superblock, and no shared-memory tile - on an
engine that ships to other people's hardware, where LDS pressure and barrier
cost are not what they are here.

THE HYPOTHESIS IT FALSIFIES, which is the more useful half. q6_k mat-vec on
this box runs at 41.0 GB/s effective at m=17408 n=3 k=5120 while q5_k at the
identical shape runs 63.8 and q4_k reaches 72.5 - a real, reproducible ~35%
deficit. The per-row barrier was the leading mechanism for it. It is not:
removing the barrier moves nothing measurable, in the gated build or in an
ungated probe build measured against clean arifi/main under the same
position-balanced discipline (q6_k median 0.988, control 0.999). The deficit
stands, unexplained, and the next attempt should look elsewhere.

Evidence lives in research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-02-lane-209/rank3-r10/ :
OPUS-5-R7-780M-KERNEL-ATTACK-REPORT.md, R7-AB-opA.json, R7-AB-serveA.json,
R7-AB-binA.json, R7-OP-ANALYSIS-opA.json, R7-OP-ANALYSIS-binA.json,
R7-SERVE-ANALYSIS.json, R7-PROFILE-base.json

Origin: ArifiLabs (native)
Measured-effect: NEUTRAL on gfx1103 - op-level q6_k median ON/OFF 0.9796 inside a
  78-row untouched control at 0.9990, p10-p90 [0.952, 1.053]; serve TIED on all
  six prefill/decode cells; output byte-identical, 1 sha per prompt over 12
  launches. No speed win is claimed.
